== /etc/fstab file based mount options hardening ==
This approach ships a hardened <code>/etc/fstab</code> by default (for new builds and installers). This is the traditional location where sysadmins expect mount options to be configured.

* Shipping a hardened by default /etc/fstab in new Kicksecure builds.
* Upgrading existing Kicksecure builds is possible too, can be considered later.
* {{Github_link|
https://github.com/Kicksecure/security-misc/blob/master/usr/share/doc/security-misc/fstab-vm
}}
* {{Github_link|
https://github.com/Kicksecure/security-misc/issues/157
}}

Advantages:

* No initrd modifications.
* No shell scripting.
* configurability: As expected by sysadmins. /etc/fstab could be used normally. No special user documentation required.
* Should be the most robust, stable place as this is traditionally the place to configure this.

Disadvantages:

* Tailored for Kicksecure such as for VM builds or installations from [[ISO]] using Calamares installer.
* Not a generic solution for security-misc.
* Needs to be contributed separately to Qubes: https://github.com/QubesOS/qubes-issues/issues/5329

Plan:

* If and only if needed: Combine this with [[#systemd kernel parameter based mount options hardening]] for the API file systems.

== remount-secure daemon ==

Implement remount-secure as a daemon that runs before <code>sysinit.target</code> and after <code>local-fs.target</code>. It will do an initial scan of file systems and remount applicable file systems and directories securely, then monitor the system's mounts and remount newly introduced file systems securely as needed. Configuration files will be used to allow adding new bind-mounts to directories that are not real mountpoints, and to allow applying more permissive mount options to certain mountpoints. The system administrator will be able to modify this configuration as desired.

Pending:

* intial scan should be separate command line option?

Advantages:

* No initrd modifications.
* Not touching /etc/fstab.
* Handles file systems introduced at runtime, not just ones present at bootup.
* Handles file systems that aren't known about while in the initrd (i.e. those for which mount units exist outside of the initrd).
* Can be used for all mount points.
* Allows sysadmin customization.

Disadvantages:

* configurability: Not as expected by sysadmins using /etc/fstab. Needs different way to customize.
* May be confusing to users who don't expect mount options to be manipulated by remount-secure.
* Requires remounting, we can't reconfigure <code>mount</code> to use more secure default mount options.
* Will override settings like <code>-o exec,dev,suid</code> unless those settings are configured in the remount-secure configuration files.
* yet another daemon, more resource consumption / RAM use, may be frustrating for Qubes
* might break UDisks2 (i.e. mounting of removable media in the file manager)? Hopefully not, needs testing
* remount-secure daemon potential race conditions
** might be a weakness, not a vulnerability
** might only be circumvented if malware with exec permission is running but without SUID access waiting for a SUID race condition
** abusing the race condition in most cases will require root
*** but if malware already has root, SUID race condition is no longer required
*** because only root can create SUID binaries that set user id to root
*** only an issue if there's already vulnerable SUID on the mounted device
** still a lot more secure than without remount-secure daemon
*** applies to newly attached file systems only
*** not applicable to file systems at boot
*** there's no better alternative than the kernel adding a feature to configure default mount options
**** todo: kernel feature request

= Default Mount Option Policy =

Defaults (maximum hardening):

* <code>nosuid</code> and <code>nodev</code>: applied wherever applicable, with inherent exceptions: <code>/dev</code> omits <code>nodev</code> because it holds device nodes; <code>/usr</code> omits <code>nosuid</code> because <code>setuid</code> programs such as <code>sudo</code> live there.
* <code>noexec</code>, in three tiers mapped onto the <code>remountsecure=</code> level:
** '''Always''' (even at the lowest active level; these never need execution): <code>/boot</code>, <code>/boot/efi</code>, <code>/dev</code>, <code>/var/log</code>. (<code>/sys</code> is already <code>nosuid,nodev,noexec</code> by default, so <code>remount-secure</code> does not remount it.)
** '''Level 2 and up''' (most data directories): <code>/run</code>, <code>/tmp</code>, <code>/dev/shm</code>, <code>/var/tmp</code>, <code>/var/cache</code>, <code>/srv</code>, <code>/media</code>, <code>/mnt</code>.
** '''Level 3 only''' (default, highest): <code>/home</code> and <code>/root</code>.
* '''Never''' <code>noexec</code>: <code>/</code>, <code>/var</code> (<code>dpkg</code> runs maintainer scripts from <code>/var/lib/dpkg/info</code>), <code>/usr</code>, <code>/etc</code>, <code>/opt</code> (legitimate executables). See [[noexec]].
* Configurable via the <code>remountsecure=</code> levels: <code>remountsecure=2</code> opts <code>/home</code> and <code>/root</code> out of <code>noexec</code>; <code>remountsecure=0</code> disables.

Implementation:

* A single systemd oneshot remounts at runtime; no <code>/etc/fstab</code> changes, so Qubes and non-Qubes are covered uniformly.
* Uses <code>rbind</code> so a directory's submounts are preserved (a plain bind would hide them).
* The <code>remountsecure=</code> kernel parameter (0 to 3) selects the level; default is 3 (highest).
* Disabled by default via systemd preset until validated on a VM, then enabled by default (<code>50-security-misc.preset</code>, {{Github_link|
https://github.com/Kicksecure/security-misc/pull/152
}}).
* Future: a daemon mode could additionally harden filesystems attached at runtime (removable media). For removable media, configuring UDisks2 mount options at mount time avoids a remount race.

= Relationship to pam_namespace =

<code>pam_namespace</code> can polyinstantiate directories (for example a per-user <code>/tmp</code> and <code>/dev/shm</code> via the <code>tmpfs</code> method). Such an instance is a fresh <code>tmpfs</code> mounted per session at login, inside that session's private mount namespace. Remount Secure runs once at boot against the global mounts and therefore cannot reach a per-session instance created later at login.

Division of responsibility (one policy, two enforcement points, never two mechanisms on the same mount):

* '''One policy source''': Remount Secure and its configuration define which directories get <code>nosuid</code>, <code>nodev</code>, and <code>noexec</code>. <code>pam_namespace</code> must not define a divergent policy.
* '''Remount Secure''' enforces that policy on global and persistent mounts, on <code>/home</code> and data directories, and owns all <code>noexec</code> decisions for those.
* '''<code>pam_namespace</code>''' enforces the same policy only on the per-session instances it itself creates, because it is the only mechanism that can reach them. It must set at least <code>nosuid,nodev</code> on those instances; if the policy enables <code>noexec</code> for <code>/tmp</code> or <code>/dev/shm</code>, the per-session instances must carry it too, since Remount Secure cannot add it afterwards.
* <code>pam_namespace</code> must not harden global or persistent mounts.
* Rationale: a single authoritative policy with clearly separated enforcement points is more maintainable than two mechanisms competing on the same mount. See [[Dev/maintainability]].

= Towards Write-xor-Execute (W^X) =

<code>noexec</code> is a useful building block but is '''not''' write-xor-execute (W^X) on its own. What it does and does not stop was tested, not assumed (reproduced on Linux 6.18 / glibc 2.41; see [[#Reproduction]] below).

What <code>noexec</code> '''does''' stop:

* <code>execve()</code> of files on the mount: native binaries and <code>#!</code> scripts.
* A <code>PROT_EXEC</code> file mapping of a file on the mount. This '''includes the dynamic-loader route''': <code>ld.so /path/binary</code> is '''blocked''', not a bypass. Verified: <code>ld.so</code> runs the same binary from an exec mount but fails on a <code>noexec</code> mount with "failed to map segment from shared object".

What <code>noexec</code> does '''not''' stop (both reproduced):

* '''Interpreters.''' An interpreter reads a script as ordinary data and runs it itself, so <code>noexec</code> never applies: <code>sh script.sh</code>, <code>python3 script.py</code>, likewise <code>perl</code>, <code>awk</code>, etc. all execute from a <code>noexec</code> directory.

{{quotation
|quote=This scenario plays out for all programs that accept dynamic code at runtime. So not only shell scripts, but also awk, Python, Perl, etc...
|source=Chromium OS security docs, https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromiumos/docs/+/HEAD/security/noexec_shell_scripts.md
}}

* '''Fileless execution.''' <code>memfd_create(2)</code> plus <code>execveat(2)</code> runs code from anonymous memory, touching no filesystem at all. Reproduced at the default <code>vm.memfd_noexec=0</code>. <code>vm.memfd_noexec=2</code> blocks this specific exec-syscall path and is now set by [[security-misc]]; the weaker <code>=1</code> is insufficient, because an attacker simply passes <code>MFD_EXEC</code>. It is a bar-raiser, '''not''' W^X: an executable mapping of the memfd is still reachable via <code>mmap(PROT_EXEC)</code>/<code>mprotect</code> (by design -- Linux has no system-wide W^X knob), and on kernels before 6.18 the execute bit could also be re-added via a POSIX ACL (<code>setfacl</code>); both are reproduced below. The ACL path was a kernel seal-coverage bug, fixed in Linux 6.18 by commit <code>0237c47ca170</code> ("mm/shmem: honour F_SEAL_EXEC when an ACL is set", <code>Cc: stable</code>); the <code>mmap</code>/<code>mprotect</code> route is inherent and remains.

{{quotation
|quote=this executable nature of memfd opens a door for NoExec bypass
|source=Linux kernel documentation, https://docs.kernel.org/userspace-api/mfd_noexec.html
}}

See also madaidan's Linux hardening guide (https://madaidans-insecurities.github.io/guides/linux-hardening.html): "Be aware that noexec can be bypassed via shell scripts."

== What the additional controls can and cannot do ==

Full W^X is a combination of controls, each with limits; there is no single global switch.

* '''AppArmor is per-program MAC, with no global W^X.''' A profile can deny the <code>m</code> permission (a <code>PROT_EXEC</code> file mapping) and executable mappings of writable paths.

{{quotation
|quote=m - allow PROT_EXEC with mmap(2) calls
|source=apparmor.d(5), https://manpages.debian.org/unstable/apparmor/apparmor.d.5.en.html
}}

: But this constrains only '''confined''' programs; an unconfined shell or interpreter is unaffected, and each application needs its own profile. So AppArmor hardens specific sensitive applications (browser, PDF and media viewers, network daemons) against write-then-execute attacks such as JIT spray or loading a writable shared object. It is '''not''' a system-wide W^X and does '''not''' stop the interpreter vector (interpreting source or bytecode is not a <code>PROT_EXEC</code> file mapping). On a <code>noexec</code> mount a <code>PROT_EXEC</code> file mapping is already refused by the mount flag, so for files on that mount the <code>m</code> denial is largely redundant with <code>noexec</code>; its value is for paths not under a <code>noexec</code> mount.
* '''The interpreter vector''' is addressed by restricting access to interpreters and compilers, tracked separately as [https://forums.whonix.org/t/lock-down-interpreters-compilers-interpreter-lock-compiler-lock/18499 interpreter lock / compiler lock], not by <code>noexec</code> and not by AppArmor <code>m</code>.
* '''The memfd exec-syscall vector''' is raised, not closed, by <code>vm.memfd_noexec=2</code>, set by [[security-misc]] (see its limits above: the <code>mmap(PROT_EXEC)</code> route is inherent and remains; the <code>setfacl</code> path was a kernel bug fixed in Linux 6.18, commit <code>0237c47ca170</code>).

Conclusion: <code>noexec</code> on writable data directories raises the bar (it stops a naive <code>execve</code> and the loader route) but is not W^X by itself. The honest answer to "pair it with AppArmor" is that AppArmor W^X is per-application and does not cover the interpreter or fileless vectors; a fuller W^X is <code>noexec</code> plus interpreter/compiler lockdown plus <code>vm.memfd_noexec=2</code> plus per-application AppArmor confinement, each with the limits above.

Note on the kernel discussion: the <code>execveat(AT_CHECK)</code> / <code>ld.so</code> thread on the kernel-hardening list (https://www.openwall.com/lists/kernel-hardening/2024/07/19/3) concerns a '''proposed''' securebits mechanism to extend exec checks into the dynamic linker and <code>mmap</code> paths. It is a design discussion, not a demonstration that the <code>noexec</code> mount flag is bypassed by <code>ld.so</code> (which, as shown above, it is not).

== Reproduction ==

The claims above were reproduced inside a private user and mount namespace (<code>unshare --map-root-user --mount</code>) on Linux 6.18, glibc 2.41, Debian trixie:

* Shell script on a <code>noexec</code> tmpfs: <code>./s.sh</code> -> Permission denied; <code>sh s.sh</code> -> runs.
* Python script on a <code>noexec</code> tmpfs: <code>python3 p.py</code> -> runs.
* ELF on a <code>noexec</code> tmpfs: <code>./myecho</code> -> Permission denied; <code>ld.so ./myecho</code> -> "failed to map segment" (blocked). Control: the same <code>ld.so ./myecho</code> on an exec tmpfs runs, confirming the only difference is the mount flag.
* <code>memfd_create(2)</code> + <code>execveat(2)</code>, tested per <code>vm.memfd_noexec</code> value on the Kicksecure kernel (Linux 6.12.111, each value in its own PID namespace): at <code>0</code> a no-flag memfd executes (the bypass); at <code>1</code> the no-flag path is denied but an explicit <code>MFD_EXEC</code> memfd still executes; at <code>2</code> <code>memfd_create(MFD_EXEC)</code> is refused at creation and a default memfd cannot be run via the exec syscalls.
* Limits of <code>vm.memfd_noexec=2</code>, also reproduced on Linux 6.12.111 (it is a bar-raiser, not W^X): on a value-2 noexec-sealed memfd, <code>mmap(PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC)</code> and <code>mmap(PROT_READ)</code>+<code>mprotect(PROT_EXEC)</code> both succeed (an executable mapping is still obtainable); <code>setfacl -m u:USER:rwx</code> on <code>/proc/PID/fd/N</code> adds the execute bit that <code>fchmod</code> refuses (on 6.12.111 <code>F_SEAL_EXEC</code> did not cover the POSIX ACL path) and the fd then executes -- this was a kernel bug, fixed in Linux 6.18 by commit <code>0237c47ca170</code> ("mm/shmem: honour F_SEAL_EXEC when an ACL is set", <code>Cc: stable</code>), so a kernel carrying that fix (or its stable backport) refuses the <code>setfacl</code>; and the default memfd is created sealable (<code>MFD_ALLOW_SEALING</code> implied), so a peer can add seals a value-0 memfd would refuse.
* Compatibility at <code>2</code>: common system tooling (<code>systemd</code>, <code>journald</code>, <code>apt</code>, <code>dpkg</code>, <code>python3</code>, <code>perl</code>, <code>gpg</code>, <code>openssl</code>) is unaffected; a child PID namespace cannot weaken a stricter host value.

